---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import PositionList from '../components/PositionList.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import CalloutPanel from '../components/CalloutPanel.astro';
import { positions } from '../data/positions';
import { sources } from '../data/sources';

const pageTitle = 'The evidence';
const pageDescription =
  'Medical-organization positions on routine infant circumcision, what the foreskin does, the health claims in proportion, the risks of the procedure, and how simple intact care actually is.';
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} pageKey="evidence">
  <section class="section section--tight evidence-head">
    <div class="container container--reading">
      <h1>The evidence</h1>
      <p class="evidence-head__lede">
        Five things worth knowing before anyone picks up a scalpel: what the
        world's medical organizations say, what the foreskin is, how the
        health claims look in absolute numbers, what the procedure risks, and
        what caring for an intact child actually involves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--content">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Section 1"
        heading="What medical organizations say"
        lede="The international picture is easy to summarize: no national medical association or health authority in the world recommends that infant boys be routinely circumcised. The disagreement between countries is only about whether parents may choose it — or whether the choice belongs to the boy himself, later."
      />
      <PositionList items={positions} />
      <p class="section-note">
        The American statement is the most favorable one on this list, and
        even it does not recommend the procedure. Several European bodies
        state plainly that the decision should wait for the person it
        affects.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading evidence-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Section 2"
        heading="What the foreskin is and does"
      />
      <p>
        The foreskin is not a defect or a leftover. It is normal, healthy
        tissue: a mobile, double-layered fold that covers and protects the
        glans, keeps its surface soft, and glides during movement. Anatomical
        studies describe specialized, richly innervated mucosa — Taylor's 1996
        work identified a distinctly innervated band near the tip, and later
        work mapped the tissue in more detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        How much circumcision changes adult sensation is genuinely debated,
        and this site will not pretend otherwise. One study (Sorrells 2007)
        found the most sensitive regions of the penis to fine touch are in
        tissue circumcision removes; another (Bossio 2016) found smaller
        differences and is often cited the other way. What is not debated is
        the anatomy: the tissue is functional, and removing it is not
        trimming something inert.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[
          sources.taylor1996,
          sources.coldTaylor1999,
          sources.sorrells2007,
          sources.bossio2016,
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading evidence-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Section 3"
        heading="The health claims, in proportion"
        lede="Most claimed benefits are real but small, or real but relevant to adults — and none of them, per any medical body, adds up to a recommendation. Absolute numbers matter more than relative percentages."
      />
      <h3>Urinary tract infections</h3>
      <p>
        Circumcision does reduce UTIs in the first year. But the baseline
        risk for an intact boy is about 1%, so a systematic review estimated
        roughly 111 circumcisions to prevent a single UTI — an infection that
        is ordinarily diagnosed and treated with antibiotics, as it is in
        girls, whose UTI rates are higher. Surgery is not how medicine
        normally prevents a treatable 1% risk.
      </p>
      <h3>Penile cancer</h3>
      <p>
        Penile cancer is very rare — on the order of 1 case per 100,000 men
        per year — and its major risk factors are HPV, smoking, and phimosis.
        The American Cancer Society discusses circumcision in its risk-factor
        pages and does not recommend it as a cancer-prevention measure.
      </p>
      <h3>HIV and other STIs</h3>
      <p>
        The strongest evidence for circumcision comes from three randomized
        trials of adult volunteers in high-HIV-prevalence African countries,
        which found reduced female-to-male transmission. That is the best
        case for the procedure, so it deserves a straight answer: those
        trials studied consenting adults in epidemiological settings very
        unlike the United States; condoms are far more protective; and a
        newborn faces no sexual transmission risk at all. Nothing about HIV
        prevention requires operating on an infant — the person the foreskin
        belongs to can weigh that evidence himself at an age when it is
        actually relevant.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[
          sources.singhGrewal2005,
          sources.aapTechReport,
          sources.acsPenileCancer,
          sources.cochraneHiv,
          sources.frisch2013,
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading evidence-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Section 4"
        heading="What the procedure risks"
        lede="Circumcision is surgery, and it has surgery's failure modes. Listed flatly, without pictures:"
      />
      <ul class="risk-list">
        <li>Bleeding and infection — the most common acute complications.</li>
        <li>Meatal stenosis — narrowing of the urinary opening, a recognized complication of circumcision and far more common in circumcised boys.</li>
        <li>Adhesions and skin bridges, which can require further procedures.</li>
        <li>Removal of too much or too little tissue; unsatisfactory results needing revision.</li>
        <li>Rare but severe injuries, including damage to the glans.</li>
        <li>
          Pain. Newborns feel it fully, local anesthesia does not eliminate
          it, and a Lancet study found circumcised infants showed stronger
          pain responses at routine vaccinations months later.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Reported complication rates vary widely with the operator, setting,
        and how long problems are tracked — the AAP's own technical report
        acknowledged the true rate is unknown. Every complication here is
        incurred to remove tissue that was healthy.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[
          sources.weiss2010,
          sources.aapTechReport,
          sources.taddio1997,
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container container--reading evidence-prose">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Section 5"
        heading="Caring for an intact child"
        lede="The claim that an intact penis is unsanitary or hard to care for has the facts exactly backwards. Intact care is the absence of a task."
      />
      <p>
        The rule, straight from the AAP's parent guidance: <strong>do not
        retract the foreskin — wipe the outside like a finger, and nothing
        more</strong>. In infancy the foreskin is naturally fused to the
        glans. It separates on its own timeline, often over years, and the
        boy himself will be the first person who can and should retract it,
        gently, in the bath.
      </p>
      <p>
        Forced retraction — by a parent or a clinician — is what causes the
        pain, tearing, and scarring that get blamed on the foreskin itself.
        You may decline it at any exam, and it is worth saying so out loud at
        checkups, because not every practice has caught up with its own
        professional guidance.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare the two newborns honestly: the intact baby needs nothing
        done; the circumcised baby has an open surgical wound healing inside
        a diaper. One of these is the easier hygiene story, and it is not the
        one the myth says.
      </p>
      <SourceList
        sources={[
          sources.healthyChildrenCare,
          sources.docCare,
          sources.ywbCare,
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CalloutPanel
    heading="Seen enough to want the short version?"
    body="The common claims — unsanitary, match dad, prevents disease, he won't remember — each get a direct answer."
    primaryLabel="Common claims, answered"
    primaryHref="/faq"
    secondaryLabel="What you can do"
    secondaryHref="/take-action"
  />
</Layout>

<style>
  .evidence-head {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }

  .evidence-head h1 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .evidence-head__lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }

  .evidence-prose p {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .evidence-prose h3 {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0 var(--space-3);
  }

  .section-note {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--color-body);
    max-width: var(--measure-reading);
  }

  .risk-list {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .risk-list li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .risk-list li::marker {
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }
</style>
